window.CHALLENGES = [{"id":"assess-cascade","type":"assess","tier":1,"title":"Which rule wins, and why it is not order","unlocks":"cascade-order","ground":"Two rules set the same property on the same element. One is written two hundred lines later than the other.","task":"Given six selector pairs, compute both specificity triples, name the winner, and name the column that decided it. Then find the pair where the loser would win under @layer and explain what changed.","rubric":[{"band":"Six correct triples","test":"Are all six correct, including the :has(), :is() and :where() cases?"},{"band":"The deciding column is named","test":"Does the answer name which of a, b or c decided it, rather than only the winner?"},{"band":"The layer case is understood","test":"Does the explanation put layers BEFORE specificity in the cascade order, rather than treating them as a tiebreak?"}]},{"id":"code-fungible","type":"code","tier":2,"title":"Write the preference function, and fail","unlocks":"fungibility","ground":"The constructive test: try to write a function that ranks two units. If it compiles, they are not fungible.","task":"Define a unit type carrying no field that can distinguish two instances. Then write compare(a, b) and demonstrate that it cannot be written without adding one. Then add the field and show what it costs.","rubric":[{"band":"It runs","test":"Does the type exist and the demonstration execute?"},{"band":"The failure is structural","test":"Is compare impossible because of the type, or merely unwritten? A comment saying \"we choose not to\" fails this band."},{"band":"The cost is shown","test":"After adding the distinguishing field, is there a working ranking — and is it obvious who would collect on it?"}]},{"id":"code-genesis","type":"code","tier":1,"title":"A genesis block with nothing in it","unlocks":"the-minimum-cycle","ground":"Every chain begins with somebody typing a first block. The question is what is in it. A premine is a preference written into the ground state, and a preference is a ranking.","task":"Write a genesis block whose allocation map is empty, hash it, and link a second block to it. Then write the test that fails if any unit exists at height 0.","rubric":[{"band":"It runs","test":"Do two blocks exist, linked, and does the link verify?"},{"band":"The test is real","test":"Does it fail when you deliberately add one unit to genesis? Prove it by adding one."},{"band":"The press is named","test":"Does the code or its comment acknowledge that somebody still pressed start? The injector does not disappear because the allocation is empty."}]},{"id":"math-forest","type":"math","tier":2,"title":"Cost the forest, and say what you assumed","unlocks":"hydrogen","ground":"90,000 square miles of forest, in hydrogen. Biomass at an assumed 200 t/ha is 4,662 Mt at ~6% hydrogen; held water at an assumed 100 kg/m² is 23,310 Mt at 11.2%. Total 2,888 Mt.","task":"Redo the calculation with your own two assumptions, show every division, and then write 150 words explaining which assumption the answer is most sensitive to and how you know.","rubric":[{"band":"The arithmetic is shown","test":"Is every figure shown as a division a reader can repeat?"},{"band":"The assumptions are labelled","test":"Are the two assumed inputs marked as assumed, in the working and not only in the prose?"},{"band":"The sensitivity is argued, not asserted","test":"Did you vary each input and report the swing, or did you reason about it in your head and state a conclusion?"},{"band":"The conclusion survives","test":"Does the shipping answer — six orders of magnitude short — hold under both of your assumptions? If it does not, say so."}]},{"id":"voice-clash","type":"voice","tier":3,"title":"The clash, and the people standing in it","unlocks":"the-prior-condition","ground":"The Carnatic Wars, south-east India, 1746–1763. Two European companies — French at Pondicherry, English at Madras — fight each other through local succession disputes, each backing a claimant, each raising and paying local troops. Neither is governing. Both are trading companies with armies. The ground they are contesting is somebody’s district, and the people on it are not the subject of the dispute; they are the terrain of it. The same shape appears on the other coast, in North America, in the same decades and with the same two companies’ governments behind it.","sources":["Carnatic Wars, 1746–1763","Anglo-French contest for the Coromandel coast; Pondichéry and Madras"],"standpoints":[{"id":"weaver","who":"A weaver in a village that changes hands twice","brief":"The buyer of his cloth changes and the price changes with it. He has no view on which company should win and a very precise view on what each one pays. Write somebody for whom this is a market condition."},{"id":"french","who":"A French officer at Pondichéry","brief":"He is outnumbered, underfunded, and better at this than his opposite number, and he knows all three. Write a professional losing slowly to a larger balance sheet."},{"id":"factor","who":"An English factor at Madras","brief":"He came out to trade and is now signing for gunpowder. Write the voice of somebody who has noticed that his job changed and has not said so in a letter home."},{"id":"both","who":"A sepoy who has served both companies","brief":"He has worn two uniforms and been paid by both, and the drill was nearly the same. He is the only person in this exercise who can compare them from inside. Do not make him cynical — make him accurate."}],"task":"Write 300–400 words in ONE voice, during a week when the district changes hands. The contest is the weather, not the subject.","rubric":[{"band":"It is a voice","test":"Could a reader identify the standpoint from any 40 consecutive words, header removed?"},{"band":"Neither company is the protagonist","test":"Could either side quote this piece approvingly? If exactly one could, it has become an argument."},{"band":"The ground is somebody’s home","test":"Is there a detail in it that only somebody who lives there would think to mention?"},{"band":"It is not an argument","test":"Remove every essayable sentence. Is there still a piece?"}]},{"id":"voice-fork","type":"voice","tier":4,"title":"The fork in the road","unlocks":"the-injector","ground":"Two documents, two years apart. In December 1830 President Jackson tells Congress that removal is generous — that it will \"separate the Indians from immediate contact with settlements of whites\" and is what a humane policy requires. In 1832, in Worcester v. Georgia, Marshall’s court holds that the Cherokee Nation is a distinct community within which Georgia law has no force. The executive does not enforce it. Removal proceeds. Nobody in this story acts outside their office and the outcome is what it is.","sources":["Jackson, Second Annual Message to Congress, 6 December 1830","Indian Removal Act, 28 May 1830","Worcester v. Georgia, 31 U.S. (6 Pet.) 515 (1832)"],"standpoints":[{"id":"president","who":"The President, drafting the message","brief":"He believes it. That is the exercise. Write a man arriving at a decision he considers merciful, using an argument he finds sound, in an office that gives him the last word and the fewest hours to use it. No wink to the reader."},{"id":"justice","who":"Marshall, two years later","brief":"He has now written both opinions. In 1823 he supplied the doctrine; in 1832 he limits it. Write somebody discovering the reach of a sentence he wrote nine years ago."},{"id":"delegate","who":"A Cherokee delegate in Washington","brief":"He has won in court and it has changed nothing. He is literate in two legal systems and both have just been demonstrated to him. Write competence meeting a wall, not helplessness."},{"id":"clerk2","who":"A War Department clerk costing the removal","brief":"Wagons, rations, per head, per mile. He is good at his job. The attribution gap again, and this time the numbers are his."}],"task":"Write 300–400 words in ONE voice on the day the decision becomes irreversible. A fork closes behind whoever takes it — find the moment it shuts, and do not have the voice notice it shutting.","rubric":[{"band":"It is a voice","test":"Could a reader identify the standpoint from any 40 consecutive words, header removed?"},{"band":"The President is not a villain","test":"If you wrote him: is the argument he makes one a reader could be persuaded by for the length of a paragraph? If not, you wrote a verdict."},{"band":"Nobody steps outside their office","test":"Does every figure act within their actual powers? An invented abuse of power is an escape from the exercise, which is about the ordinary use of it."},{"band":"The fork is visible and unremarked","test":"Can a reader see the point of no return that the voice cannot?"}]},{"id":"voice-rename","type":"voice","tier":1,"title":"The rename","unlocks":"the-rename","ground":"Johnson v. M'Intosh, US Supreme Court, 1823. A title dispute over land in what is now southern Illinois. Thomas Johnson's heirs held land bought directly from Piankeshaw sellers in 1773 and 1775; William M'Intosh held a later federal patent on overlapping ground. Chief Justice Marshall, for a unanimous court, held that discovery had vested title in the discovering European nation, that the United States had inherited it, and that native nations retained only a 'right of occupancy' — which meant they could not sell to a private buyer, because they had never held the thing being sold. Nobody was dispossessed by the ruling on the day it was handed down. The land had been renamed, and the renaming was the whole of it.","sources":["Johnson & Graham’s Lessee v. M’Intosh, 21 U.S. (8 Wheat.) 543 (1823)","Marshall, C.J., for a unanimous court"],"standpoints":[{"id":"marshall","who":"Marshall, writing the opinion","brief":"He is not gloating and he is not lying. He believes he is describing a settled state of affairs rather than creating one, and the opinion goes out of its way to call the doctrine 'opposed to natural right' while applying it anyway. Write the voice of a man who knows exactly what he is doing and has decided it is already done."},{"id":"seller","who":"A Piankeshaw signatory to the 1775 sale","brief":"He signed something. Forty-eight years later a court in a city he has never seen rules that what he signed could not have been what he thought it was. Write him without making him a symbol — he had reasons, he had a price, and he was not naive."},{"id":"mcintosh","who":"M'Intosh, holding the federal patent","brief":"He bought from the government. He is, on the facts, the innocent party in a title dispute, and the ruling that vindicates him rests on a doctrine he did not invent and probably has not read. Write the voice of somebody being handed a windfall he considers ordinary."},{"id":"clerk","who":"The clerk copying the decision into the reports","brief":"He is paid by the page. He will read this sentence more carefully than anybody in the courtroom did, and he has no standing to say anything about it. The attribution gap, from inside."}],"task":"Write 300–400 words in ONE of these voices, at a specific hour on a specific day. No summary of the case, no verdict on it, no anachronism. The voice must know things the other three do not, and must not know things they do.","rubric":[{"band":"It is a voice","test":"Could a reader identify the standpoint from any 40 consecutive words, with the header removed?"},{"band":"It is that voice and not a costume","test":"Does it contain at least one thing this person would find ordinary that the reader will find remarkable — and is it left unremarked?"},{"band":"It knows its own limits","test":"Is there something the voice is wrong about, or cannot see, that the piece does not step outside itself to correct?"},{"band":"It is not an argument","test":"Remove every sentence that could appear in an essay about the case. Is there still a piece left?"}]},{"id":"voice-retainer","type":"voice","tier":2,"title":"The retainer","unlocks":"the-retainer","ground":"The subsidiary alliance system, British India, from 1798 under Wellesley. A ruler keeps his throne, his title, his court and his revenues. He accepts a British force stationed in his territory, pays for it, accepts a Resident at his court, and gives up the conduct of his own foreign relations. There is no conquest, no annexation, and on paper no loss of sovereignty. The instrument contains no false statement anywhere, which is why it survived scrutiny for fifty years. He was not deposed. He was retained.","sources":["Subsidiary alliance treaties, 1798 onward (Hyderabad 1798, Awadh 1801, others)","Wellesley’s despatches as Governor-General"],"standpoints":[{"id":"nawab","who":"The ruler who signed","brief":"He kept everything he could name and lost something he cannot. Write him at a moment when the arrangement is working exactly as agreed and he can find nothing to object to."},{"id":"resident","who":"The Resident at his court","brief":"Polite, competent, genuinely fond of the man, and structurally his replacement. He would be offended to be called a governor and he is one."},{"id":"sepoy","who":"A soldier in the subsidiary force","brief":"He is paid out of the ruler’s treasury and takes orders from the Company. By 1857 there are roughly six of him for every British soldier — 135,000 Indian to 24,000 European in the Bengal Army alone. Write him on an ordinary day, not a famous one."},{"id":"farmer","who":"A revenue farmer in the district","brief":"The demand went up because the force has to be paid for. Nobody lied to him and nothing was stolen. Write the voice of somebody doing arithmetic that comes out wrong."}],"task":"Write 300–400 words in ONE voice, on a day when nothing happens. The hard version of this exercise is that there is no villain in the room and the piece still has to land.","rubric":[{"band":"It is a voice","test":"Could a reader identify the standpoint from any 40 consecutive words, header removed?"},{"band":"Nothing is stolen in it","test":"Does the piece resist inventing a crime? If a theft or a lie appears, the exercise has been avoided rather than done."},{"band":"The instrument is visible","test":"Can a reader reconstruct the shape of the arrangement from the voice alone, without it being explained to them?"},{"band":"It is not an argument","test":"Remove every sentence that could appear in an essay. Is there still a piece?"}]},{"id":"write-negative","type":"write","tier":2,"title":"State a negative so a reader can check it","unlocks":"the-zero-counter","ground":"A sentence saying \"no opinion carries a drafter’s name\" is a claim a reader has to take. A counter named `named`, declared, incremented by nothing, and printed, is the same claim in a form they can watch fail to move.","task":"Find a negative claim in your own work — something you assert is absent. Build the instrument that lets a reader verify the absence without trusting you, then write 200 words on why the sentence was not enough.","rubric":[{"band":"The instrument exists","test":"Can a reader operate it without reading the source?"},{"band":"It could move","test":"Is there a path by which the number WOULD change? A counter nothing could ever increment proves nothing."},{"band":"The prose does not repeat the instrument","test":"Does the writing say something the counter cannot?"}]}];
